# A kit

A **kit** is the format's word for a reusable library you import. On the locked
model it's nothing special: **a kit is a plain Elixir module that exports a prefix
of related helpers.** When it declares a `@behaviour`, that behaviour is the shared
interface. No WIT, no manifest — the public functions are the exports.

`FinModel` is our money vocabulary: `ltv/1`, `cac/1`, `npv/2`, authored once so
three copies of the same formula never drift across the workbook.

## The interface — an `@behaviour`

server :finmodel do
  defmodule Deal do
    defstruct lead: nil, acquisition_cost: 0, cash_flows: [], discount_rate: 0.12
  end

  defmodule FinModel do
    @callback ltv(Lead.t()) :: number()
    @callback cac(Deal.t()) :: number()
    @callback npv(Deal.t(), float()) :: number()
  end
end

The `@callback`s document the interface; the real types are the `%Lead{}` and
`%Deal{}` structs, inferred from their defaults. Structure, not a schema language.

## The implementation

`Default` adopts the behaviour, so the compiler checks all three callbacks exist —
a missing one fails at the weave gate, not in production.

server :finmodel do
  defmodule Default do
    @behaviour FinModel

    @impl FinModel
    def ltv(%Lead{revenue: revenue, employees: employees}) do
      annual = (revenue * 0.10 + employees * 1_200) * 0.80
      Float.round(annual * 3.0, 2)
    end

    @impl FinModel
    def cac(%Deal{acquisition_cost: cost}) when cost >= 0, do: cost * 1.0

    @impl FinModel
    def npv(%Deal{cash_flows: flows}, rate) when is_float(rate) do
      flows
      |> Enum.with_index(1)
      |> Enum.reduce(0.0, fn {flow, year}, acc -> acc + flow / :math.pow(1 + rate, year) end)
      |> Float.round(2)
    end
  end
end

Author the formula once, behind a `@behaviour`, and a customer can drop in their
own without forking ours. Next: [[Using a kit]].
